// Detection: RDP Logon From a Device That Doesn't Normally Use It
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1021.001 (Remote Services: Remote Desktop Protocol)
// Log source: Microsoft Defender XDR — DeviceLogonEvents
//
// What it detects:
//   Successful interactive/remote-interactive logons over RDP where the
//   source device has no RDP logon history to that target in the
//   preceding 14 days — a proxy for "new lateral movement path."
//
// Why it matters:
//   RDP remains one of the most common lateral movement channels in
//   both ransomware and targeted intrusions because it's a trusted,
//   already-open protocol in most Windows environments. A brand-new
//   source-to-target RDP path is exactly the kind of signal that's easy
//   for a human analyst to miss but cheap for KQL to flag.
//
// False positive considerations:
//   - New IT support staff, new employee onboarding, or first-time
//     access to a newly built server will all show up as "new" paths —
//     this detection is best used to build a watchlist for review, not
//     as a stand-alone high-severity page.
//   - Jump-box / bastion host architectures will legitimately show many
//     new source-target pairs; exclude your designated jump-box device
//     names from the "source" side.

let LookbackWindow = 14d;
let KnownPairs =
    DeviceLogonEvents
    | where Timestamp between (ago(LookbackWindow) .. ago(1d))
    | where LogonType in ("RemoteInteractive")
    | where ActionType == "LogonSuccess"
    | summarize by DeviceName, RemoteIP;
DeviceLogonEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(1d)
| where LogonType in ("RemoteInteractive")
| where ActionType == "LogonSuccess"
| join kind=leftanti KnownPairs on DeviceName, RemoteIP
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, RemoteIP, AccountName, LogonType, ActionType
| order by Timestamp desc
